   "id": "mZr8TLucAAAJ",
   "from": "Christian Dorion",
   "author": "c5a3e043bd4e7830",
   "date": "2015-10-30T10:42:34Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Poisson jumps with time-varying intensity",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks Bob. Yes, I estimated the GARCH model in the examples and it worked. This is an extension in which I simply provide the optimal parameters as data (from lambda to hy1) in order to focus on estimating the y&#39;s.<div><br></div><div>Your point regarding nJ is news to me. I thought that </div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nJ</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>nJ  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> poisson</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">hy</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br><br></span></div></code></div><br>would simply sample nJ from a poisson distribution, but I understand from your comment that it does not. That&#39;s probably what cause my problem as nJ indeed seems to be always zero given the sampled y&#39;s. Is it possible to actually simulate poisson variables nJ[tn] of intensity hy[tn] at each time step tn?</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Thursday, October 29, 2015 at 8:58:06 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">I&#39;m not familiar with th emodel you&#39;re trying to estimate\r\n<br>and those are a lot of variables to take in.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Have you tried estimating something simpler and working\r\n<br>up to something of this complexity?\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Was your intention to just do the forward sampling for y?\r\n<br>There aren&#39;t any other parameters in the model.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Whatever the case, your model block won&#39;t work because you\r\n<br>declare a local variable and then use it before defining it:\r\n<br>\r\n<br> int nJ[T];\r\n<br>  eps ~ normal(0,1);\r\n<br>  nJ  ~ poisson(hy);\r\n<br>\r\n<br>The sampling statement does not define a variable.  It assumes\r\n<br>the variable is already defined and uses it to increment the overall\r\n<br>log density for the posterior.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>- Bob\r\n<br><br></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
