   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Oct 30, 2015, at 6:42 AM, Christian Dorion &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">christia...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Thanks Bob. Yes, I estimated the GARCH model in the examples and it worked. This is an extension in which I simply provide the optimal parameters as data (from lambda to hy1) in order to focus on estimating the y&#39;s.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Your point regarding nJ is news to me. I thought that \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; int nJ[T]; \r<br>&gt; nJ  ~ poisson(hy); \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; would simply sample nJ from a poisson distribution, but I understand from your comment that it does not. That&#39;s probably what cause my problem as nJ indeed seems to be always zero given the sampled y&#39;s. Is it possible to actually simulate poisson variables nJ[tn] of intensity hy[tn] at each time step tn?\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Yes, but not in a way so as to have them interact\r<br>with the model.  If you put the nJ generation in the\r<br>generated quantities block and use poisson_rng(hy), you\r<br>can generate Poisson random values.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob"
    ]
   ]
